import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Backlog","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/BACKLOG.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/BACKLOG.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/BACKLOG.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="backlog" tabindex="-1">Backlog <a class="header-anchor" href="#backlog" aria-label="Permalink to “Backlog”">​</a></h1><h2 id="tooling-deferred-—-self-hosting-see-project-rfcs" tabindex="-1">Tooling (deferred — self-hosting, see <code>project/rfcs/</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#tooling-deferred-—-self-hosting-see-project-rfcs" aria-label="Permalink to “Tooling (deferred — self-hosting, see project/rfcs/)”">​</a></h2><p><em>Not now (2026-10-18): RFC-0001 stays parked until the language and compiled backends stabilize.</em></p><p>The ADR-0085..0094 bootstrap tooling implementation is near-term work and tracked in <code>TODO.md</code>. Everything below is the <em>low priority</em> self-hosting follow-up: porting the tooling to handl itself once the language is stable. The generic Rust substrate (plugin system, <code>plugin-ext-*</code>, <code>plugin-engine-*</code>) stays as bootstrap and fallback (ADR-0085 rule 6).</p><ul><li><p><strong>RFC-0001 — Plugin system in handl.</strong> Extract the Rust plugin system (<code>platform</code> crate: Capability/Plugin/Kit/Host) into handl: capabilities and plugins as values, kits as module lists, hosts as handlers.</p></li><li><p><strong>RFC-0002 — Formatter in handl.</strong> Refactor of bootstrap <code>plugin-ext-fmt</code>; formatter as a pure arrow, rules compose by <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>, idempotence as a law.</p></li><li><p><strong>RFC-0003 — Linter in handl.</strong> Refactor of bootstrap <code>plugin-ext-lint</code>; rules are pure arrows <code>Ast ~&gt; [Diagnostic]</code>, <code>--fix</code> as a fixpoint pipeline, cross-module rules over the module graph.</p></li><li><p><strong>RFC-0004 — Typechecker in handl.</strong> Refactor of bootstrap <code>plugin-engine-typecheck</code>; adds the deferred scope (traits, refinements, invariants, row variables). Blocked on compiled backends (perf).</p></li><li><p><strong>RFC-0005 — Test runner in handl.</strong> Refactor of bootstrap <code>plugin-engine-test</code>; adds parallel execution (ADR-0072), properties, benchmarks.</p></li><li><p><strong>RFC-0006 — LSP server in handl.</strong> Hardest target, intentionally last; session as a fold over the message stream, features as arrows, thin Rust transport adapter. Blocked on RFC-0004 perf.</p></li><li><p><strong>RFC-0007 — CLI in handl with tool-integration plugins.</strong> Realizes ADR-0029: generic, composable, extensible, replaceable, modular, documented CLI that loads integrations contributed by the tool RFCs and is decoupled from them.</p></li><li><p><strong>VS Code extension.</strong> The <code>editor-support/vscode</code> skeleton exists; full feature work is deferred until RFC-0006 LSP-in-handl matures.</p></li></ul><p>Order: RFC-0001 → 0002/0003 → 0005 → 0007 → 0004 → 0006.</p><h2 id="compiler-backends" tabindex="-1">Compiler backends <a class="header-anchor" href="#compiler-backends" aria-label="Permalink to “Compiler backends”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>CommonJS output for <code>hnl compile js</code></strong> — <code>JsOutputFormat</code> currently emits ESM only. Deferred: ESM covers the node/browser targets in use; revisit if a CommonJS-only embedder appears. (<code>web</code> repo, <code>modules/compile-js</code>.)</li></ul><h2 id="arrow-macros-adr-0084" tabindex="-1">Arrow macros (ADR-0084) <a class="header-anchor" href="#arrow-macros-adr-0084" aria-label="Permalink to “Arrow macros (ADR-0084)”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Ref: <a href="./../project/adrs/0084-arrow-macros.html">ADR-0084 — Arrow Macros</a></li><li>Type-safe compile-time metaprogramming. Macros are arrows (<code>Ast ~&gt; Ast</code>), typechecked before expansion. No new calculus primitives.</li><li>Subsumes <code>const</code> (ADR-0083 §5.1) and <code>data</code> (ADR-0082) as degenerate macros. Enables derivation (<code>derive_show</code>, <code>derive_eq</code>) and DSL embedding (SQL, regex, HTML).</li><li>Low priority — not blocking ADR-0083 (FFI-Native Stdlib). <code>const</code> + <code>prim.or</code> cover the immediate POSIX constants need.</li></ul><h2 id="lib-utils" tabindex="-1">lib-utils <a class="header-anchor" href="#lib-utils" aria-label="Permalink to “lib-utils”">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>Add non-crypto hashes — hash.fnv1a(input), hash.murmur3(input, seed?), hash.xxhash(input, seed?). Fast non-cryptographic hashes returning numbers (not hex digests). For sharding, bucket keys, dedup. NOT in lib-crypto (not security-related). Low priority — build only if needed.</p></li><li><p>Add matrix — 2D operations (transpose, multiply, determinant, identity, inverse). Useful but niche; only if handl users actually need linear algebra.</p></li><li><p>Add encode.* — encode.base64(data), encode.hex(data), decode.base64(str). Common in automation for handling binary data, tokens. Pure compute, lib-utils.</p></li><li><p>Add log.* — log.info(msg), log.warn(msg), log.error(msg), log.debug(msg). Structured output to stderr. Platform-dependent (writes to stdout/stderr), fits in lib-platform + lib-platform-std.</p></li><li><p>serialize.* — JSON/YAML/TOML parse/stringify (but serde_json may suffice inline)</p></li><li><p>stream.* — streaming/chunked data processing</p></li><li><p>format / template — string interpolation. Two designs: (A) <code>str.format(template, values)</code> as a pure function in lib-utils (fits existing pattern), or (B) scope-resolving interpolation in the runtime layer. See notes/format-template.md for details.</p></li></ul><h2 id="lib-graph" tabindex="-1">lib-graph <a class="header-anchor" href="#lib-graph" aria-label="Permalink to “lib-graph”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Add graph theory production-ready bindings/lib. feature complete.</li></ul><h2 id="test-framework-primitives-adr-0092-unblockers" tabindex="-1">Test framework primitives (ADR-0092 unblockers) <a class="header-anchor" href="#test-framework-primitives-adr-0092-unblockers" aria-label="Permalink to “Test framework primitives (ADR-0092 unblockers)”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Add <code>time.sleep_ms</code> to <code>handl-packages/time</code> (medium priority — deferred from the initial <code>time</code> contract; effectful, belongs behind a capability, not needed by <code>benchmark</code>/<code>property</code>/<code>fuzz</code>).</li><li>Strengthen <code>rand.seeded_*</code> contract guarantees: add a determinism predicate (e.g. <code>Deterministic</code>/<code>Eq</code>) to <code>plugin-ext-refinement</code>&#39;s <code>Pred</code> vocabulary so &quot;same seed + args ⇒ same output&quot; is a real <code>guarantee:</code> rather than a <code>-- LAW:</code> comment.</li></ul><h2 id="lib-utils-combinators" tabindex="-1">lib-utils (combinators) <a class="header-anchor" href="#lib-utils-combinators" aria-label="Permalink to “lib-utils (combinators)”">​</a></h2><ul><li>AST-based inlining — <code>substitute_param</code> uses byte-level string scanning, not AST-based substitution. Correct for all tested cases but theoretically fragile with exotic string escapes. See notes/ast-based-inlining.md.</li></ul><h2 id="lib-re-—-regular-expressions" tabindex="-1">lib-re — Regular Expressions <a class="header-anchor" href="#lib-re-—-regular-expressions" aria-label="Permalink to “lib-re — Regular Expressions”">​</a></h2><ul><li>New crate <code>lib-re</code> for pattern matching, backed by Rust&#39;s <code>regex</code> crate.</li><li>Keeps <code>str</code> simple; puts pattern complexity behind an opt-in module.</li><li>See notes/lib-re.md for API details.</li></ul><h2 id="lib-fuzzy-—-approximate-string-matching" tabindex="-1">lib-fuzzy — Approximate String Matching <a class="header-anchor" href="#lib-fuzzy-—-approximate-string-matching" aria-label="Permalink to “lib-fuzzy — Approximate String Matching”">​</a></h2><ul><li>New crate <code>lib-fuzzy</code> for fuzzy string matching beyond basic <code>str.levenshtein</code>/<code>str.similarity</code>.</li><li>Jaro-Winkler, n-gram, Damerau-Levenshtein, fuzzy search/filter.</li><li>See notes/lib-fuzzy.md for API details.</li></ul><h2 id="lib-text-—-text-processing" tabindex="-1">lib-text — Text Processing <a class="header-anchor" href="#lib-text-—-text-processing" aria-label="Permalink to “lib-text — Text Processing”">​</a></h2><ul><li>New crate <code>lib-text</code> for higher-level text operations (word count, tokenization, n-grams, dedent, indent).</li><li>Does not belong in <code>str</code> (too high-level) or <code>intl</code> (not locale-aware).</li><li>See notes/lib-text.md for API details.</li></ul><h2 id="lib-diff-—-string-diffing" tabindex="-1">lib-diff — String Diffing <a class="header-anchor" href="#lib-diff-—-string-diffing" aria-label="Permalink to “lib-diff — String Diffing”">​</a></h2><ul><li>New crate <code>lib-diff</code> for character/word/line-level diffing and patch application.</li><li>See notes/lib-diff.md for API details.</li></ul><h2 id="module-system-async-operation-dispatch" tabindex="-1">Module system: async + operation dispatch <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-system-async-operation-dispatch" aria-label="Permalink to “Module system: async + operation dispatch”">​</a></h2><p>The VM (<code>handl-vm</code>) supports three dispatch channels via <code>Host</code>: sync <code>Function</code>, <code>AsyncFunction</code>, and <code>Operation</code>. But <code>Module</code> trait and <code>ModuleHost</code> only wire sync <code>Function</code>. This blocks all lib-* crates from exposing async or operation-based capabilities.</p><ul><li><strong>P0: Extend <code>Module</code> + <code>ModuleHost</code></strong> — add <code>async_function()</code> and <code>operation()</code> to <code>Module</code> (default <code>None</code>), implement <code>Host::async_function</code> and <code>Host::operation</code> on <code>ModuleHost</code>. Unblocks everything below.</li><li><strong>P1: <code>http</code>/<code>fs</code>/<code>cmd</code> async + operations</strong> — add async variants to platform traits; expose I/O operations as <code>Operation</code> for handler interception, permission control, cross-machine resumability.</li><li><strong>P2: <code>fp.map</code>/<code>filter</code>/<code>reduce</code> async</strong> — async higher-order functions using <code>ctx.eval_expr_async</code> (<code>AsyncEval</code> callback).</li><li><strong>P3: <code>kv</code> async</strong> — async <code>KeyValueStore</code> trait for network backends (Redis, SQLite).</li><li><strong>P3: <code>DefaultHost</code> expansion</strong> — more built-ins beyond <code>add</code>/<code>mul</code>/<code>concat</code>/<code>len</code>.</li></ul><p>See notes/module-async-operations.md for details.</p><h2 id="tree-shaking-dead-code-elimination" tabindex="-1">Tree-shaking / Dead code elimination <a class="header-anchor" href="#tree-shaking-dead-code-elimination" aria-label="Permalink to “Tree-shaking / Dead code elimination”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Crate-level: DONE</strong> — <code>handl</code> facade is tree-shakeable via Cargo features (22 feature flags, <code>optional = true</code> deps, <code>default</code> = everything for backwards compat).</li><li><strong>Language-level: NOT DONE</strong> — no DCE pass exists. All declared fields are computed; no reachability analysis. A <code>DeadFieldElimPass</code> could slot into the compiler pipeline via the <code>Pass</code> trait. See notes/tree-shaking.md for design and challenges.</li></ul><h2 id="platform-plugin-security" tabindex="-1">Platform plugin security <a class="header-anchor" href="#platform-plugin-security" aria-label="Permalink to “Platform plugin security”">​</a></h2><p>Security is <strong>out of scope for the initial platform refactor</strong> (see <code>docs/adr/0002-handl-platform-composable-host.md</code>). Future work, in rough priority order:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Sandboxed <code>Target</code> plugins.</strong></p><ul><li><code>WasmTarget</code> plugin that compiles generated <code>handl</code> code to WebAssembly and runs it in a VM.</li><li><code>InterpreterTarget</code> plugin with an in-process, capability-limited interpreter.</li><li>The <code>Host</code> and operation plugins stay native; only the generated user code is sandboxed.</li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Plugin permission manifest.</strong></p><ul><li><code>Plugin</code> manifest can declare required permissions: <code>register-plugin</code>, <code>network</code>, <code>filesystem</code>, <code>process</code>, etc.</li><li><code>Host</code> enforces declared permissions before loading a plugin.</li><li><code>ContributionContext</code> is restricted based on the loaded plugin&#39;s permission set.</li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Signed kits and libraries.</strong></p><ul><li>Kit publishers sign manifests.</li><li><code>handl-host-cli</code> refuses to load a kit with a missing or invalid signature.</li><li>Key distribution and revocation policy (TBD).</li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Reproducible, hash-pinned dependency trees.</strong></p><ul><li><code>Kit</code> manifests pin exact plugin versions and dependency hashes.</li><li>Host validates the loaded dependency tree against the pinned hashes before execution.</li></ul></li></ol><h2 id="self-hosted-host-and-meta-circular-composition" tabindex="-1">Self-hosted <code>Host</code> and meta-circular composition <a class="header-anchor" href="#self-hosted-host-and-meta-circular-composition" aria-label="Permalink to “Self-hosted Host and meta-circular composition”">​</a></h2><p>The new plugin model is intentionally minimal. This makes it possible, eventually, to write the <code>Host</code> itself in <code>handl</code> rather than Rust. Long-term research direction, not part of the initial refactor:</p><ol><li><p><strong><code>handl</code> as a host implementation language.</strong></p><ul><li>Write <code>Host</code>, <code>PluginRegistry</code>, <code>ContributionContext</code>, and the <code>Plugin</code> model in <code>handl</code>.</li><li>The <code>handl</code> program compiles to a native or WASM target and behaves the same as the Rust <code>Host</code>.</li><li>Still requires a minimal Rust bootstrap runtime that can load and run the compiled handl <code>Host</code>.</li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Plugins that manage the <code>Host</code>.</strong></p><ul><li>A <code>HostFactory</code> plugin can create and configure new <code>Host</code> instances.</li><li>A <code>Host</code> can spawn isolated child hosts with a subset of plugins, useful for tests, workers, and sandboxed compilation units.</li><li>The <code>Host</code> itself becomes a capability that language-level code can request and compose.</li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Security through the <code>handl</code> language.</strong></p><ul><li><code>handl</code> is designed for sandboxing and capability tracking.</li><li>Once the <code>Host</code> is written in <code>handl</code>, capabilities (<code>contribute</code>, <code>register-plugin</code>, <code>execute</code>) can be checked by the language runtime.</li><li>User <code>handl</code> code can safely spawn and inspect sub-hosts because the language enforces effect and permission boundaries.</li></ul></li></ol><h2 id="polyglot-module-syntax" tabindex="-1">Polyglot module syntax <a class="header-anchor" href="#polyglot-module-syntax" aria-label="Permalink to “Polyglot module syntax”">​</a></h2><p>The decoupled parser/lexer/formatter model makes it possible to load modules written in different syntaxes at runtime. This is not part of the initial platform refactor.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Pluggable <code>Parser</code> by file extension / syntax marker.</strong></p><ul><li><code>handl-ext-parser</code> accepts <code>Behavior&lt;Parser&gt;</code> rules keyed by a syntax id.</li><li>A plugin like <code>handl-plugin-yaml-syntax</code> contributes a <code>yaml</code> parser.</li><li>The <code>Parser</code> plugin dispatches to the correct <code>Behavior</code> based on file extension or an inline <code>syntax</code> declaration.</li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Pluggable <code>Lexer</code> and <code>Formatter</code> per syntax.</strong></p><ul><li>Each syntax plugin can also contribute <code>Behavior&lt;Lexer&gt;</code> and <code>Behavior&lt;Format&gt;</code> for its own token and output rules.</li><li><code>BasicLexer</code> and <code>BasicFormatter</code> dispatch by syntax id before dispatching by <code>AstNode.kind</code>.</li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Modules with their own language.</strong></p><ul><li>A module can declare <code>syntax &quot;my-dsl&quot;</code>.</li><li>The host loads the corresponding syntax plugin if not already present.</li><li>The module is parsed to the common <code>AstNode</code> shape and participates in the normal pipeline.</li></ul></li></ol><h2 id="host-tensor-lib-tensor" tabindex="-1">host-tensor / lib-tensor <a class="header-anchor" href="#host-tensor-lib-tensor" aria-label="Permalink to “host-tensor / lib-tensor”">​</a></h2><p>The pair is at 28 ops with 11 registered deriv pairs (every non-constructor op has an adjoint); lib-tensor mirrors all 28 and verifies 45/45 differentially under <code>hnl-run</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>dtype beyond <code>f64</code></strong> — kernel slice first: <code>handl-value</code>&#39;s <code>TensorValue</code> is <code>Vec&lt;f64&gt;</code>; a second dtype needs an enum payload + codec there (same shape the <code>Tensor</code> variant took). Then here: <code>Tensor.data</code> becomes the enum and each op picks a rule — same-dtype with an error on mixing is the likely default (promotion lattices are where the bugs live). The wire field is already reserved (<code>&quot;dtype&quot;: &quot;f64&quot;</code>; decode rejects everything else today).</li><li><strong><code>.hnl</code>-function autodiff</strong> — <code>tensor.derive</code> is a registered-pairs table, deliberately not autodiff: a host <code>Function</code> sees only evaluated argument values, so a <code>.hnl</code> arrow&#39;s name resolves to <code>null</code>. Real autodiff on <code>.hnl</code> functions needs engine machinery (source-to-source transform or a tape in eval) — an engine slice, not a host-tensor one.</li></ul><h2 id="type-system-surface-sugar" tabindex="-1">Type-system surface sugar <a class="header-anchor" href="#type-system-surface-sugar" aria-label="Permalink to “Type-system surface sugar”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong><code>exists m. T</code> existential sugar</strong> — surface spelling that lowers to the <code>Sigma(Nat, m ~&gt; T)</code> encoding (explicit <code>Sigma</code> is the decision; sugar deferred). Covers <code>filter</code>-style return types (<code>Vec{?m, a}</code> — <code>?m</code> index holes don&#39;t lex today) and Sigma destructure ergonomics (pair pattern on the intro <code>[m, v]</code>; the destructured length var currently reads as the <code>Nat</code> sort inside <code>Vec(m, a)</code> — bare index names resolve through gamma to their sort, only compound terms like <code>m + 0</code> stay <code>var</code>).</li></ul>`,46)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};